<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · 1891 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Click Go the Shears</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Australian folk song about a sheep shearer's long day in the shed, from the days before machines did the shearing.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Henry Clay Work (the tune of &quot;Ring the Bell, Watchman&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Australian sheep shearers and the wool industry</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bacchus Marsh district, Victoria, Australia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first published in 1891; popular from the 1950s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; first published in 1891)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/click-go-the-shears">Click Go the Shears</a>&quot; is a traditional Australian song about a day's work for a sheep shearer in the days before machine shearing. Its lasting popularity reflects the long place of the wool industry in Australian life.</p><p>In June 2013 the folklorist Mark Gregory found that a version was first published in 1891 in a regional Victorian newspaper, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a> Marsh Express, under the title &quot;The Bare Belled Ewe&quot;, to be sung to the tune of &quot;Ring the Bell, Watchman&quot;. It was signed &quot;C. C. Eynesbury, Nov. 20, 1891&quot;; Eynesbury was a rural property in the Bacchus Marsh area, and &quot;C.C.&quot; may have been the author. There was a shearers' strike in 1891, so the song would have resonated with Australians when it appeared. The tune is that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> song &quot;Ring the Bell, Watchman&quot; by Henry Clay Work, and the first verse follows Work's words closely, as a parody.</p><p>The song was next printed in 1939 in two Australian newspapers, and in 1946 the musicologist the Reverend Dr Percy Jones published it as a traditional song he had &quot;collected and arranged&quot;. It became popular only in the 1950s, more than half a century after it first appeared. Its words vary widely from version to version, and one coarse line is often softened or rewritten.</p><p>The American folk singer Burl Ives recorded it in 1952 for his album Australian Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a>, the British folklorist A. L. Lloyd in 1956 and the American singer William Clauson in 1958. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> replaced the pound with the dollar in 1966, a jingle for the changeover was written to its tune. In 1973, when Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gough-whitlam">Gough Whitlam</a> visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, it was played through loudspeakers along his motorcade's route from Peking International Airport to his hotel. Olivia Newton-John sang it in her television special Olivia Down Under in 1988, and in January 2014 Chloe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> Roweth sang the 1891 version for an ABC TV story.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A picture of work in a shearing shed: the shearers at their pace, the man in charge of the floor, a newcomer learning the trade, and a boy who tends the sheep's cuts. At the end of the day an old shearer takes his pay to the pub. The mood is brisk and good-humoured.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Click goes his shears; click, click, click.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Wide are the blows, and his hand is moving quick,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The ringer looks round, for he lost it by a blow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And he curses that old shearer with the bare belled ewe.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; the opening verse as first published in 1891, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Click_Go_the_Shears" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
